From 8112c503a029c3963be62517ed5a32f7cb082e4c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Thomas Steiner <steiner.thomas@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 10:51:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Implement the Cross-Origin Storage (COS) API

Adds navigator.crossOriginStorage, a browser-wide content-addressable cache
that lets a byte-identical resource fetched once by one origin be reused by
another, keyed by cryptographic hash rather than by URL.

Spec:      https://wicg.github.io/cross-origin-storage/
Explainer: https://github.com/WICG/cross-origin-storage

Renderer: a thin IPC client behind the CrossOriginStorage runtime flag. It
runs the Permissions Policy gate before "validate a COS request", so a
policy-blocked context sees NotAllowedError even for input that would
otherwise be a TypeError, and it owns no state.

Browser: CrossOriginStorageRegistry, per BrowserContext, owns every spec
algorithm -- the pending/written entry state machine, the three disclosure
scopes, availability gating, hash verification, visibility upgrades, quota
and eviction, and rate limiting. Nothing arriving over the pipe is trusted:
hashes and origins are re-validated there because a compromised renderer can
speak the interface directly and the hash becomes a filesystem path. The
origin is taken from the browser's own record of the binding context, never
from the message.

COS entries are handed to script as ordinary FileSystemFileHandles by
implementing the File System Access mojo interfaces over a COS-backed file
system. Only getFile() and createWritable() are meaningful for an entry with
no name and no parent, so the structural operations are rejected. Writes go
to a disk-backed swap file and are hashed once over the finished contents at
close(), since seek()/truncate() can invalidate bytes already written.

Availability gating for "*"-scoped entries consults the real Public Hash
List, added under third_party/ as 311,494 sorted 32-byte digests (~9.5 MiB)
re-packed from upstream's ~58 MB text form and shipped as a loose data file
loaded once at registry startup. See that directory's README.chromium for
the roll process, including what is not yet automated.

Implementation choices the spec deliberately leaves open follow the
cross-vendor engineering notes accompanying the proposal, so that
independent implementations do not diverge needlessly.

Not included: the declarative HTML, CSS and import-attribute integrations,
which are defined in their own host-language specifications; and the
DevTools and extension surfaces for the implementation-private provenance
records, whose storage is in place but has no consumer yet.

Bug: none yet
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
